A page carries the component factories it builds instances from, and no other
page's.

Every window here renders a `Log`, whose reactive loop makes each row a
runtime instance, so each page needs the factory for it. The three pages from
the loop share one: the loop is lowered once, and optimize.Documents unrolls
the pages after lowering, one at a time. `about` is written on its own and has
a factory of its own.

A static site's page used to be written from the whole package, and carried
every factory and render slot the package held -- on the docs site, eighty
pages each carrying eighty copies of the search box's, a build that grew with
the square of the page count. The loop's pages were unrolled before lowering
then, so each had a Log of its own and every page held four.

deny out/none/html/one.html `__cf___child[1-9]` -- one.html builds only the loop's Log
deny out/none/html/two.html `__cf___child[1-9]` -- two.html builds only the loop's Log
deny out/none/html/three.html `__cf___child[1-9]` -- three.html builds only the loop's Log
deny out/none/html/about.html `__cf___child0\b` -- about.html builds only its own Log
deny out/none/html/about.html `__renderSlot1\b` -- the loop pages' render slot is not about.html's

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output { none { html } }

struct Page {
    slug string = ""
    title string = ""
}

const pages list<Page> = [
    {slug="one", title="One"},
    {slug="two", title="Two"},
    {slug="three", title="Three"},
]

component Log(label = "") node {
    var lines list<string> = []
    button(text=label, @click { lines.push(label) })
    for var line = lines {
        text(value=line)
    }
}

for var p = pages {
    window(title=p.title, href="/" + p.slug + ".html") {
        Log(label=p.title)
    }
}

window(title="About", href="/about.html") {
    Log(label="About")
}
